(Summary: The Transformer Architecture and Self-Attention) Tj
T* (Reading group handout - Week 2) Tj
T* (1. Motivation) Tj
T* (Recurrent networks process tokens one at a time, which makes training slow and makes it) Tj
T* (hard to learn dependencies between distant words. The Transformer drops recurrence entirely) Tj
T* (and relies on attention to relate every position in a sequence to every other position.) Tj
T* (2. Scaled dot-product attention) Tj
T* (Each token is projected into a query Q, a key K and a value V. The output is) Tj
T* (softmax\(Q K^T / sqrt\(d_k\)\) V. Dividing by the square root of the key dimension keeps the) Tj
T* (dot products from growing too large, which would push the softmax into tiny gradients.) Tj
T* (3. Multi-head attention) Tj
T* (Instead of one attention function, the model runs h heads in parallel on smaller) Tj
T* (projections and concatenates the results. Different heads learn different relations,) Tj
T* (for example syntax in one head and coreference in another.) Tj
T* (4. Positional encoding) Tj
T* (Attention has no notion of order, so sine and cosine signals of different frequencies) Tj
T* (are added to the input embeddings to encode each token's position.) Tj
T* (5. Encoder and decoder) Tj
T* (The encoder stacks self-attention and feed-forward layers with residual connections and) Tj
T* (layer normalization. The decoder adds masked self-attention so a position cannot look at) Tj
T* (future tokens, plus cross-attention over the encoder output.) Tj
T* (6. Cost) Tj
T* (Self-attention is quadratic in sequence length, which motivates later work on sparse) Tj
T* (and linear attention variants.) Tj
T* (Discussion questions) Tj
T* (- Why does the model need both residual connections and layer normalization?) Tj
T* (- What is lost by replacing recurrence with attention?) Tj
ET
